WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.139
Yep, that's me when I was 10.

2
00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:11.119
I loved animals, handicrafts, and dreaming.

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:15.439
Back then, I was the belligerent queen of anti-math.

4
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:22.360
I neglected, ignored, flunked, and downright hated math and science all through grade school,

5
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.840
middle school, and high school.

6
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.479
It's strange to realize I'm now a professor of engineering.

7
00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:34.439
I enlisted in the Army right out of high school to study language at the Defense Language

8
00:00:34.439 --> 00:00:35.439
Institute.

9
00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:42.479
That's me at 18, looking very nervous and very focused, while throwing a hand grenade.

10
00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:48.000
I only started to study math and science when I was 26 years old, after I got out of the

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.439
military.

12
00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:51.799
At first, it was really hard.

13
00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:56.000
There were all these quick thinkers in my classes who seemed to get everything a lot

14
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.400
easier and faster than me.

15
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.880
Sometimes I'd take a break for a few months.

16
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.760
I'd go out and work as a Russian translator on Soviet trawlers.

17
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.559
That's me up in the Bering Sea.

18
00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:10.760
Then I'd come back to school and try and learn some more.

19
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.040
As I gained technical know-how, new doors started opening up for me.

20
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.360
I ended up working as a radio operator at the South Pole Station in Antarctica.

21
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.360
That's where I met my husband.

22
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.879
I always say I had to go to the end of the earth to meet that man.

23
00:01:26.879 --> 00:01:34.239
There he is after only 10 minutes outside at minus 70 degrees Fahrenheit with a 60 mile

24
00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:35.919
an hour wind.

25
00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:39.040
The wind chill takes it off the charts.

26
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.959
Now, I wasn't natural in math and science.

27
00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:44.160
Not at all.

28
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.120
The way I succeeded was to gradually begin to figure out some tricks.

29
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.599
But let's back up a step.

30
00:01:52.599 --> 00:01:58.199
In the greater scheme of all the different careers and disciplines that people can pursue,

31
00:01:58.199 --> 00:02:04.400
why are those involving math and science sometimes a bit more challenging?

32
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.199
We think it may be related, at least in part, to the abstract nature of the ideas.

33
00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:16.759
I mean, let's take a cow, for example, outstanding in a field.

34
00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:24.160
If you have the word cow, you can point right to a cow to learn what that word means.

35
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.720
Even the letters for the word cow, C-O-W, are roughly analogous to sounds that they

36
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.360
stand for.

37
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:38.160
But for mathematical ideas, there's often no analogous thing you can point to.

38
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.160
There are no plus signs standing out in a field, no multiplication, division, or other

39
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.679
kinds of things that can directly equate to many mathematical or scientific terms.

40
00:02:50.679 --> 00:02:53.960
These terms are more abstract, in other words.

41
00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:00.800
Well, you might say, yeah, but what about words like love or zest or hope?

42
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.279
Those are all abstract.

43
00:03:02.279 --> 00:03:04.520
Yes, they are.

44
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:10.639
But the thing is, these abstract terms are often related to our emotions.

45
00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:17.039
We can feel our emotions even if we can't see and point to concrete examples like we

46
00:03:17.039 --> 00:03:19.199
could with the cow.

47
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:23.479
This means it's important to practice with ideas and concepts you're learning in math

48
00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:28.679
and science, just like anything else you're learning, to help enhance and strengthen the

49
00:03:28.679 --> 00:03:32.800
neural connections you're making during the learning process.

50
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.279
You can see on your left here the symbolic representation of a thought pattern.

51
00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:43.440
Thought patterns become linked together through repeated use.

52
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.360
The more abstract something is, the more important it is to practice in order to bring those

53
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.880
ideas into reality for you.

54
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.399
Even if the ideas you are dealing with are abstract, the neural thought patterns you

55
00:03:58.399 --> 00:04:02.360
are creating are real and concrete.

56
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.919
At least they are if you build and strengthen them through practice.

57
00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:10.479
Here's a way to picture what's going on.

58
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:16.440
When you first begin to understand something, for example, how to solve a problem, the neural

59
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:23.000
pattern you form is there, but very weak, kind of like the faint pattern of our pinball

60
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.440
machine analogy here.

61
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.519
When you solve the problem again, fresh from the start, without looking at the solution,

62
00:04:31.519 --> 00:04:37.279
you begin deepening that neural pattern, kind of like the darker pattern you see here.

63
00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:42.600
And when you have the problem down cold, so you can go over each step completely and

64
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.519
concisely in your mind without even looking at the solution, and you've even had practice

65
00:04:48.519 --> 00:04:55.600
on related problems, why then the pattern is like this dark, firm pattern.

66
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.200
Practice makes permanent.

67
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.959
When you're learning, what you want to do is study something.

68
00:05:01.959 --> 00:05:04.320
Study it hard by focusing intently.

69
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.920
Then take a break, or at least change your focus to something different for a while.

70
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.119
During this time of seeming relaxation, your brain's diffuse mode has a chance to work

71
00:05:16.119 --> 00:05:21.239
away in the background and help you out with your conceptual understanding.

72
00:05:21.239 --> 00:05:26.200
Your neural mortar, in some sense, has a chance to dry.

73
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:32.440
If you don't do this, if instead you learn by cramming, your knowledge base will look

74
00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:39.600
more like this, all in a jumble, with everything confused, a poor foundation.

75
00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:46.320
If you have problems with procrastination, that's when you want to use the Pomodoro,

76
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.160
that brief timer.

77
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.799
This helps you get going, using brief periods each day of focused attention that will help

78
00:05:53.799 --> 00:05:58.359
you start building the neural patterns you need to be more successful in learning more

79
00:05:58.359 --> 00:06:00.720
challenging materials.

80
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:08.480
Next up, we'll be talking about chunking, the vital essence of how you grasp and master

81
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.200
key ideas.

82
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.040
I'm Barbara Oakley.

83
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.839
Thanks for learning about learning.